Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Finger Finds 94 Volts
- Story & Context: The opener begins at 100 BPM with nothing but güiro, compact kick, and a two-note prepared-piano figure. Lucía touches the Trautonium strip below the intended pitch and slowly drags upward while increasing pressure. The tone becomes simultaneously louder, brighter, and more harmonically complex until it reaches the target exactly as the snare enters. That single gesture becomes the track’s hook. Diego answers with a fretless bass slide that arrives slightly late on the tonic, while Valentina repeats the same fixed piano dyad every four bars. In the middle section, Matías removes the drums for eight bars but Mariela’s güiro continues unchanged. Lucía keeps the entire section alive through alternating pressure rather than new notes. The track ends when she lifts her finger from the strip mid-glide, leaving immediate silence before one final dry piano knock.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close documentary image of a fingertip pressing a long black resistive strip beside an analog voltmeter reading 94, prepared-piano hammers and güiro texture visible in shallow focus, fluorescent classroom light. No full lyrics; optional spoken fragments limited to “ninety-four,” “hold the pressure,” “do not quantize it,” and “lift now.”

Track 2: The Güiro Keeps the Room Open
- Story & Context: Matías programs the sparsest beat on the EP: one kick, one rim, one clap, and several intentional empty spaces. Mariela plays a continuous downward güiro rasp across the gaps, then changes to short upward strokes when Valentina inserts metallic prepared-piano octaves. Lucía uses only two structural pitches but never reaches either the same way twice: one note slides upward, one falls from above, another arrives through a fast pressure stab. Diego remains almost completely straight, outlining roots without decorative fills so the continuous Trautonium movement has a stable floor. Halfway through, every electronic channel mutes except the Trautonium. The güiro remains acoustic and audible in the room microphone, making the performance feel as if the electricity failed but the dance did not. The machine returns without fill or count-in.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long classroom aisle after a partial power failure, drum-machine LEDs dark while one musician continues scraping a metal güiro under emergency fluorescent light, Trautonium strip and upright piano in the background. Spoken fragments limited to “keep the room open,” “machine went dark,” “scrape through it,” and “come back without warning.”

Track 3: No Key Exists Between These Two Marks
- Story & Context: The closer is built around a narrow interval physically marked on Lucía’s strip controller with two small pieces of white tape. Matías writes a minor-key cumbia loop whose keyboard parts never leave equal temperament. Lucía, however, spends most of the track between the taped reference pitches, sustaining unstable intermediate tones and slowly pressing harder until upper subharmonics emerge. Valentina reinforces the contradiction with dry fixed-pitch piano triads. Diego begins each bass phrase exactly on pitch, then gradually introduces small upward slides as the piece develops. During the final passage, the rhythm simplifies to kick and güiro while Lucía moves continuously between the two tape marks without settling on either. She finally removes her finger. Valentina plays both corresponding piano notes together, revealing the rigid interval that the lead line had spent the entire track refusing to choose.
